      "PermaSand is the bag most people leave a Home Depot with, and for a straightforward patio with joints in the normal range it is a perfectly good choice. It handles gaps from roughly an eighth of an inch to an inch, cures to a firm joint that keeps weeds and ants out, and costs less per bag than the premium alternatives. What it asks in return is discipline during installation. The watering step is where PermaSand separates careful installers from disappointed ones: too little and the binders never fully activate, leaving soft joints that wash out in the first storm, and too much floods the polymer out of the joint and onto the paver faces, where it dries as a haze that is genuinely difficult to remove. Follow the bag directions on the shower setting and the number of passes rather than eyeballing it. The other point worth knowing is that PermaSand is less tolerant of dust on the paver surface than the premium sands, so the sweep-and-blow step before watering matters more here than it does with a product like Gator Maxx. On smooth, non-porous concrete pavers this is rarely an issue. On tumbled, textured or porous stone, take the extra ten minutes with a leaf blower on low before the hose comes out.",
  },
  {
    id: "quikrete-powerloc",
    name: "Quikrete PowerLoc Jointing Sand",
    category: "Standard Joint Width, 50 lb Bag",
    badge: "Best Value Per Pound",
    rating: 4.1,
    reviewCount: 1800,
    pros: [
      "50 lb bags cover more area per dollar than 40 lb competitors",
      "Widely stocked and easy to reorder mid-project",
      "Good colour range for typical concrete pavers",
      "Sets firm and resists washout once properly cured",
      "Forgiving in narrow joints where thicker sands bridge",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Heavier bags are harder to handle on a long job",
      "Not rated for the widest joints or flagstone gaps",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best coverage per dollar on the shelf. If your joints are standard width and your budget matters, this is the one to load onto the cart.",
    description:
      "PowerLoc is Quikrete's polymeric jointing sand and the value play among the in-store options, mainly because it comes in 50 lb bags where much of the competition sells 40. On a large patio that difference adds up quickly. Performance is broadly comparable to PermaSand: it fills and locks joints in the standard range, cures firm, and keeps weeds and insects out of the gaps. It flows into narrow joints particularly well, which makes it a good match for tightly laid concrete pavers where a coarser sand tends to bridge across the top of the gap instead of filling it to depth. As with every polymeric sand, the failure modes are all in the process rather than the product. The surface must be bone dry before the bag is opened, the sand must be swept in and compacted properly so the joint is filled to within a few millimetres of the paver surface, and every trace has to come off the paver faces before watering. Where PowerLoc is less suitable is very wide joints and irregular flagstone, where the manufacturer's own limits apply and a wide-joint formulation is the correct purchase instead. Check the bag for the stated joint range before buying for anything unusual.",
  },
  {
    id: "alliance-gator-maxx-g2",
    name: "Alliance Gator Maxx G2",
    category: "Premium, Wide Joints, 50 lb Bag",
    badge: "Best for Wide Joints",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1400,
    pros: [
      "Handles joints up to about four inches — flagstone territory",
      "Noticeably more haze-resistant than budget sands",
      "More tolerant of imperfect watering technique",
      "Stays flexible enough to resist cracking through freeze-thaw",
      "Available through Home Depot's site even where stores skip it",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Frequently online-only rather than on the shelf",
      "Costs meaningfully more per bag than PermaSand or PowerLoc",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one to order if your joints are wide, your stone is irregular, or you have been burned by haze before. Worth the premium on anything but a simple patio.",
    description:
      "Gator Maxx G2 is the product most installers reach for when the job is not a simple grid of concrete pavers, and it is the strongest performer of the sands you can get through Home Depot — though usually through the website rather than off the shelf. Two things justify the premium. First, joint width: it is rated for gaps up to around four inches, which brings irregular flagstone and wide-jointed natural stone into range, where the standard sands are limited to roughly an inch. Second, haze resistance. The binder system is less prone to depositing on the paver surface during watering, which removes the single most common way a polymeric sand job goes visibly wrong. It is also more tolerant of a slightly heavy hand with the hose, which matters if this is your first time using the product. None of that makes preparation optional — the surface still has to be dry, the joints still have to be filled to depth, and the paver faces still have to be swept and blown clean before watering. But the margin for error is wider than with the budget bags, and on an expensive stone patio that margin is cheap insurance. If your local store does not stock it, ordering online for delivery or store pickup is usually straightforward.",
  },
  {
    id: "dominator-polymeric-sand",
    name: "DOMINATOR Polymeric Sand",
    category: "Premium, Standard to Wide Joints",
    badge: "Best Colour Retention",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 900,
    pros: [
      "Strong colour range that holds up rather than fading grey",
      "Very low haze in practice, even on textured pavers",
      "Cures firm while staying flexible through freeze-thaw",
      "Good coverage per bag on standard joints",
      "Frequently available for delivery even in smaller markets",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Almost always an online order rather than an in-store pickup",
      "Premium pricing that is hard to justify on a small patio",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pick when the joints are part of the look rather than just a gap to fill. Order it in advance — this is not a same-day purchase.",
    description:
      "DOMINATOR sits at the premium end of what Home Depot lists, and it earns that position on appearance more than on raw performance. The colour range is broader than the mainstream bags and, more importantly, the colours hold rather than drifting towards a uniform grey after a couple of seasons — which matters when the joint pattern is a visible part of the design rather than something you want to disappear. In practice it is also among the cleanest sands to work with: haze is uncommon even on textured pavers, and the cured joints stay firm without becoming brittle through freeze-thaw cycling. The practical catch is availability. It is essentially an online product in most markets, so it needs ordering days in advance rather than being collected on the morning of the job — which is exactly the situation where people give up and buy whatever is on the shelf instead. If the patio is large, visible, and worth doing once, it is worth the planning. If it is a small utility path behind the garage, the in-store options will serve you perfectly well and save both the money and the wait.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Lists", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Polymeric Sand at Home Depot", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/polymeric-sand-home-depot/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does Home Depot sell polymeric sand?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Most stores stock Sakrete PermaSand and Quikrete PowerLoc in the masonry or paver aisle, usually in tan and grey. Premium brands such as Alliance Gator Maxx and DOMINATOR are generally listed online for delivery or store pickup rather than kept on the shelf, so they need ordering a few days ahead.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does polymeric sand cost at Home Depot?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Standard in-store bags typically run around $25 to $40 for 40 to 50 pounds, with premium brands ordered online costing meaningfully more per bag. Price per bag is the wrong comparison on its own — check the stated coverage, because a heavier bag rated for wider joints covers less area than the number suggests.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many bags of polymeric sand do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For standard narrow joints on concrete pavers, one 50 lb bag typically covers 50 to 100 square feet. Wide joints, irregular flagstone, or thick pavers can cut that to 20 to 30 square feet per bag. Always buy one bag more than the calculation suggests — running out mid-job leaves joints curing at different times and often looking different afterwards.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best polymeric sand at Home Depot?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a standard concrete paver patio bought in-store, Sakrete PermaSand or Quikrete PowerLoc both do the job, with PowerLoc offering better coverage per dollar in 50 lb bags. For wide joints, irregular flagstone, or if haze worries you, order Alliance Gator Maxx G2 online instead — it is the strongest performer in the range.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is polymeric sand from a landscape supplier better?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Often, yes — a dedicated hardscape supplier carries the full professional range including wide-joint and permeable formulations, and staff who install this material daily. For a standard patio the big-box options are perfectly adequate. For irregular stone, unusual joint widths, or a large investment in pavers, the trip is worth it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use regular sand instead?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You can, and it was standard practice for decades, but plain joint sand washes out in heavy rain, blows out under a leaf blower, grows weeds and gets excavated by ants. Polymeric sand costs more up front and solves all four. What you must never do is use play sand, which is rounded and cannot lock up at all.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you put polymeric sand over existing sand?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not directly. The old joint sand has to be removed to a depth of at least an inch and a half, ideally more, so the new material has depth to bond within. Sweeping polymeric sand on top of existing sand produces a thin crust that cracks and lifts within a season.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What happens if it rains after applying polymeric sand?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Rain within the first few hours can wash uncured binder out of the joints and leave soft, patchy areas that need redoing. Check the forecast for a clear 24 hours before starting, and if rain arrives unexpectedly soon after watering, expect to top up and re-water the affected joints once everything has dried out.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
