        <h2 id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          What You Are Actually Buying
        </h2>

        <p>
          Polymeric sand is graded joint sand with binders blended through it. Swept into the gaps between pavers and then watered, those binders activate and cure, turning loose sand into a firm but slightly flexible joint. That single change solves the four problems plain joint sand has: it washes out in heavy rain, blows out under a leaf blower, offers weed seeds a perfect seedbed, and gives ants somewhere easy to excavate.
        </p>

        <p>
          What it is not is mortar. A cured polymeric joint stays slightly flexible, which is deliberate — a paver field is supposed to move a little with the ground beneath it, and a rigid joint would crack. It is also not a structural element: the interlock between pavers comes from the joints being properly filled to depth, not from the binder holding them together like glue. That distinction explains why filling the joints only halfway produces a patio that fails even though the visible surface looks finished.
        </p>

        <p>
          The colour question comes up more than the chemistry does. In-store bags are limited to tan and grey, which suit most machine-made concrete pavers and clash with a good deal of natural stone. Colour also shifts slightly as the joints cure and again as they weather, so the shade in the bag is not exactly the shade you end up with. If matching matters, buy a single bag first, do a small area at the edge of the patio, and look at it dry the next day before committing to the rest.
        </p>

        <h2 id="joint-width" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          3. Joint Width Decides Everything
        </h2>

        <p>
          Every bag states a joint width range, and it is the specification that determines whether the job works. Standard sands cover roughly an eighth of an inch to an inch — the normal gap on machine-made concrete pavers. Wide-joint formulations extend to several inches, which is what irregular flagstone and natural stone need.
        </p>

        <p>
          Use a standard sand in a wide joint and it lacks the body to hold together across the gap: it cracks, sinks, and washes out. Use a wide-joint sand in a very narrow gap and the coarser grain bridges across the top instead of filling to depth, leaving a void underneath that collapses later. Measure a few joints before you buy, and buy for the widest ones.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
          <table class="w-full text-left">
            <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
