          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="how-many-bags" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. How Many Bags You Need
        </h2>

        <p>
          Coverage depends on joint width, joint depth and paver thickness, which is why bag figures vary so widely. As a working guide, a 50 lb bag covers <strong>50 to 100 square feet</strong> of standard narrow joints on typical concrete pavers. Wide joints or thick flagstone can drop that to <strong>20 to 30 square feet</strong> per bag.
        </p>

        <p>
          Buy one bag more than the calculation suggests. Running out partway through is worse than the wasted money, because the joints you finish later cure on a different schedule and often end up looking slightly different. Our <a href="/sand-calculator/">paver sand calculator</a> will do the arithmetic from your dimensions, and covers bedding sand as well.
        </p>

        <p>
          A related quantity question: the joints need filling to within a few millimetres of the paver surface, not to the top. Fill flush and the sand washes over the faces during watering; leave them under-filled and the joint has no strength. Sweep in, compact with a plate compactor or by tamping, then top up and sweep again before the hose comes out.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">What Else to Put in the Cart</h3>
        <p>
          The sand is rarely the only thing the job needs, and a second trip mid-installation is how dry surfaces stop being dry. A stiff-bristled push broom does the sweeping far better than a soft yard brush, which skates the sand across the faces instead of dropping it into the joints. A leaf blower — one you can throttle down, or hold well back — is what clears the paver faces before watering, and it is the single most important tool for avoiding haze. A hose nozzle with a genuine shower setting matters more than it sounds, because a jet setting will flood the joints no matter how carefully you work.
        </p>
        <p>
          For anything larger than a small path, a plate compactor is worth the rental. It settles the sand into the joints far more thoroughly than tamping by hand, which is what lets you top up and reach a properly filled joint rather than a bridged one. Most stores rent them by the half day, and the same machine is what you would have wanted for the bedding layer anyway.
        </p>

        <h2 id="store-vs-supplier" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. Big Box vs Landscape Supplier
        </h2>

        <p>
          For a standard concrete paver patio, the big-box options are genuinely fine, and the convenience of buying sand, a compactor rental and a leaf blower in one trip has real value.
        </p>

        <p>
          A dedicated hardscape supplier becomes worth the trip in three cases: when your joints fall outside the standard range, when you need a permeable formulation for a driveway designed to drain, or when the colour matters and tan and grey will not do. You will also get advice from people who install this material daily, which is worth more than the price difference on a large job.
        </p>

        <p>
          The third route is ordering the premium product online for store pickup, which gets you Gator Maxx or DOMINATOR without a separate journey. It only works if you plan a few days ahead, which is the recurring theme of this whole page.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Getting It Right on the Day
        </h2>

        <p>
          Polymeric sand fails for process reasons far more often than product reasons, and the process is unforgiving in one specific way: the surface must be completely dry when the sand goes down, and completely clean before the water goes on.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sweep the sand into the joints with a stiff broom, working diagonally across the pavers so it drops in rather than skating across. Compact, top up, and repeat until the joints are filled to a few millimetres below the surface. Then sweep the faces clean and go over them with a leaf blower on a low setting held at a shallow angle — this is the step that prevents haze, and skipping it is why so many first-time jobs end up with a milky film across the pavers.
        </p>

        <p>
          Water exactly as the bag directs, usually in several passes with a shower setting rather than one soaking. Watch for water pooling on the surface, which means you are ahead of what the joints can absorb. Our full walkthrough is in <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">how to use polymeric sand</a>, and the failure modes are catalogued in <a href="/polymeric-sand-installation-mistakes/">polymeric sand installation mistakes</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">When Polymeric Sand Is the Wrong Purchase</h3>
        <p>
          Three situations argue against it entirely, and knowing them before you are standing in the aisle saves both money and rework.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A permeable installation.</strong> If the patio or driveway was designed to drain through its joints — a permeable paver system laid over open-graded stone — a standard polymeric sand defeats the entire point by sealing the surface. There are permeable jointing products made specifically for these systems, and they are not what a big-box store stocks.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A surface that is already moving.</strong> Pavers that rock underfoot, sunken areas that hold water, or a field that has shifted out of pattern all point at a base problem. New joint sand of any kind is cosmetic there. The base has to be lifted and rebuilt, and doing that after sanding the joints means doing the sanding twice.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Very narrow, tight joints on cut stone.</strong> Where gaps are barely a joint at all, a fine plain sand often serves better than a polymeric product that cannot reach depth. The alternatives are covered in our <a href="/polymeric-sand-alternatives/">polymeric sand alternatives</a> guide, which is worth a look before defaulting to the standard bag.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Pro Tip: Check the Forecast, Not the Sky</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
            Polymeric sand needs a dry surface going down and a clear window of several hours after watering. Rain during that window flushes uncured binder out of the joints and leaves soft patches you will be redoing. A dry morning with showers forecast for the afternoon is not a day to open the bag.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Standard Bag for Wide Joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The in-store sands top out around an inch. In a wider gap they crack and wash out within a season. Measure your joints before the trip, and order a wide-joint formulation if they exceed the range on the bag.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Blowing the Faces Clean Before Watering</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Any dust left on the pavers turns into haze the moment water hits it. A leaf blower on low at a shallow angle takes five minutes and prevents the most common visible failure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Over-Watering</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flooding the joints washes the binder out and onto the surface. Follow the pass count on the bag, use a shower setting, and stop when water starts standing rather than soaking in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sweeping It Over Existing Joint Sand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The old sand has to come out to at least an inch and a half of depth first. A thin polymeric crust over loose sand cracks and lifts, usually before the first winter is out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Exactly Enough</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage figures are ranges, and joints are never uniform. One spare bag costs less than the mismatch you get when half the patio is finished on a different day.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Home Depot sell polymeric sand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes — most stores keep Sakrete PermaSand and Quikrete PowerLoc in the masonry or paver aisle, usually in tan and grey. Premium brands like Alliance Gator Maxx and DOMINATOR are generally listed online for delivery or store pickup rather than stocked on the shelf.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">In-store bags typically run around $25 to $40 for 40 to 50 pounds, with premium brands ordered online costing more. Compare coverage rather than price per bag — a heavier bag rated for wide joints covers less area than its weight suggests.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How many bags do I need?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Roughly one 50 lb bag per 50 to 100 square feet for standard narrow joints on concrete pavers, dropping to 20 to 30 square feet per bag for wide joints or thick flagstone. Buy one more than the calculation suggests so the whole patio cures on the same day.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best one they sell?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">For a standard concrete paver patio bought in-store, PowerLoc edges it on coverage per dollar and PermaSand is equally capable. For wide joints, irregular flagstone, or if haze has burned you before, order Alliance Gator Maxx G2 online — it is the strongest performer in the range.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a landscape supplier better?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Often, yes — a hardscape supplier carries the full professional range including wide-joint and permeable formulations, plus staff who install it daily. For a standard patio the big-box bags are perfectly adequate; for irregular stone or a large paver investment, make the trip.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use regular sand instead?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">You can, and people did for decades, but plain joint sand washes out in heavy rain, blows out under a leaf blower, grows weeds and hosts ants. Polymeric sand costs more and solves all four. Never use play sand — rounded grains cannot lock up at all.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put it over existing sand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Not directly. Remove the old joint sand to at least an inch and a half of depth so the new material has body to bond within. Swept over existing sand it forms a thin crust that cracks and lifts within a season.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What if it rains afterwards?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Rain within the first few hours washes uncured binder out and leaves soft, patchy joints. Look for a clear 24-hour window before starting; if rain does arrive early, expect to top up and re-water the affected joints once everything has dried.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

